1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,777
Hi, I'm Faizan Khan, and this is my

2
00:00:02,777 --> 00:00:05,897
franchise report for the Data Premier League.

3
00:00:06,177 --> 00:00:09,619
I'll walk you through the whole project: exploring the

4
00:00:09,619 --> 00:00:12,997
data, choosing a team fairly, diagnosing its problem,

5
00:00:12,997 --> 00:00:16,758
auditing the franchise's decisions, and what I'd recommend.

6
00:00:17,458 --> 00:00:19,645
The brief asks for a report on one

7
00:00:19,645 --> 00:00:21,703
team, and it hides two problems.

8
00:00:21,983 --> 00:00:25,747
Problem A: which team? If I pick a team I already have a

9
00:00:25,747 --> 00:00:29,645
story about, I'll just find that story. So the team has to

10
00:00:29,645 --> 00:00:33,610
come out of an analysis that treats all ten sides the same.

11
00:00:33,890 --> 00:00:36,495
Problem B: for that team, what's wrong, what

12
00:00:36,495 --> 00:00:39,159
did the franchise do about it, and what next?

13
00:00:39,859 --> 00:00:42,776
For both, I followed the same six steps, and

14
00:00:42,776 --> 00:00:46,025
wrote my hypotheses down before testing anything.

15
00:00:46,725 --> 00:00:50,560
This is the problem map. Money buys a squad, the squad

16
00:00:50,560 --> 00:00:54,253
plays three phases, phases produce runs, runs become

17
00:00:54,253 --> 00:00:57,733
wins, and value per crore feeds the next auction.

18
00:00:58,013 --> 00:01:01,170
Every analysis here measures one of these arrows.

19
00:01:01,870 --> 00:01:05,718
The data is every ball of 2023 and 2024: almost

20
00:01:05,718 --> 00:01:10,467
thirty-five thousand deliveries, plus both auction sheets.

21
00:01:11,167 --> 00:01:13,621
Some of it can't be taken at face value.

22
00:01:13,901 --> 00:01:16,780
The batter-out flag is true on almost every

23
00:01:16,780 --> 00:01:19,726
row, retired hurt was coded as a wicket, and

24
00:01:19,726 --> 00:01:22,605
running totals are recorded after the ball.

25
00:01:22,885 --> 00:01:25,896
So I rebuilt the match state before each delivery,

26
00:01:25,896 --> 00:01:29,029
with a guard that stops any model seeing the future.

27
00:01:29,729 --> 00:01:32,225
It's all in R, and one command rebuilds

28
00:01:32,225 --> 00:01:34,913
every number and chart from the raw files.

29
00:01:35,613 --> 00:01:39,069
Part one: before choosing anyone, I explored the whole league.

30
00:01:39,769 --> 00:01:42,807
Scoring rose by about half a run an over in

31
00:01:42,807 --> 00:01:46,339
2024, but the shape of an innings stayed the same.

32
00:01:47,039 --> 00:01:50,389
The rise wasn't even: the powerplay and the death went up

33
00:01:50,389 --> 00:01:53,738
three quarters of a run, the middle overs less than half.

34
00:01:54,438 --> 00:01:57,731
The middle overs belong to spin, and even in 2024

35
00:01:57,731 --> 00:02:01,158
it was about a run an over cheaper than pace there.

36
00:02:01,858 --> 00:02:04,728
Grounds differ by almost two runs an over, so

37
00:02:04,728 --> 00:02:07,853
any team comparison has to hold the ground fixed.

38
00:02:08,553 --> 00:02:12,674
Ball tracking adds texture: against spin, runs go to midwicket,

39
00:02:12,674 --> 00:02:16,467
and the hardest matchup is spin turning away from the bat.

40
00:02:17,167 --> 00:02:20,332
Across all twenty team-seasons, the habit

41
00:02:20,332 --> 00:02:23,342
most tied to winning was taking wickets

42
00:02:23,342 --> 00:02:26,661
in the middle overs, a correlation of 0.69.

43
00:02:27,361 --> 00:02:30,241
That gave me four rules for a fair comparison.

44
00:02:30,521 --> 00:02:32,291
Compare within each season.

45
00:02:32,571 --> 00:02:34,150
Hold the ground fixed.

46
00:02:34,430 --> 00:02:36,478
Split by phase and bowler type.

47
00:02:36,758 --> 00:02:40,150
And only trust a weakness that shows up in both seasons.

48
00:02:40,850 --> 00:02:42,440
Part two: choosing the team,

49
00:02:42,440 --> 00:02:44,541
through three gates fixed in advance.

50
00:02:45,241 --> 00:02:48,986
Gate one: did the team win fewer games than its runs

51
00:02:48,986 --> 00:02:53,091
deserved, in both seasons? Only Bengaluru and Punjab did.

52
00:02:53,791 --> 00:02:57,333
Gate two: a cause on the field. I split each team's

53
00:02:57,333 --> 00:03:00,666
game into twelve cells, by discipline, phase and

54
00:03:00,666 --> 00:03:04,069
bowler type, each measured against the other nine

55
00:03:04,069 --> 00:03:07,402
teams with ground and match situation taken out.

56
00:03:08,102 --> 00:03:11,563
A cell counts only if it survives a multiple-testing

57
00:03:11,563 --> 00:03:15,889
correction, a separate ground test, and shows up in both seasons.

58
00:03:16,169 --> 00:03:19,257
Of eighty-three cells, exactly one cleared

59
00:03:19,257 --> 00:03:22,419
all three: RCB's middle-overs spin bowling.

60
00:03:23,119 --> 00:03:26,585
Gate three is money: RCB's squad cost more than its

61
00:03:26,585 --> 00:03:30,458
wins were worth, the most negative surplus in the league.

62
00:03:31,158 --> 00:03:35,660
Here's the dashboard's overview. Only RCB passes all three

63
00:03:35,660 --> 00:03:40,395
gates; Punjab passes two. So the data chose the team, not me.

64
00:03:41,095 --> 00:03:44,031
And RCB isn't a bad team. Their powerplay

65
00:03:44,031 --> 00:03:47,110
and death bowling rank first. They have one

66
00:03:47,110 --> 00:03:50,333
hole, in exactly the phase that matters most.

67
00:03:51,033 --> 00:03:54,560
To measure all this I built four ladders of

68
00:03:54,560 --> 00:03:58,498
models, trained on 2023 and tested on 2024. Each

69
00:03:58,498 --> 00:04:02,190
time, the simplest well-calibrated model won.

70
00:04:02,890 --> 00:04:08,007
Squad wins above replacement, built ball by ball, track actual

71
00:04:08,007 --> 00:04:12,959
wins at 0.75, which puts runs, wins and rupees on one scale.

72
00:04:13,659 --> 00:04:16,578
Part four: what's wrong with RCB. I wrote

73
00:04:16,578 --> 00:04:19,569
eight hypotheses for them, before testing.

74
00:04:20,269 --> 00:04:23,089
First, is it the ground? Hold Chinnaswamy

75
00:04:23,089 --> 00:04:26,391
fixed, and their overall bowling gap disappears.

76
00:04:27,091 --> 00:04:30,374
What's left is spin in overs seven to fifteen: the

77
00:04:30,374 --> 00:04:33,854
worst in the league, and just as bad in both seasons.

78
00:04:34,554 --> 00:04:37,946
That cost about a hundred and five runs a season, a

79
00:04:37,946 --> 00:04:41,338
little over a win, worth around seven crore rupees.

80
00:04:42,038 --> 00:04:45,295
Partly it's deployment: the good options got less

81
00:04:45,295 --> 00:04:48,286
than a quarter of the overs. But no frontline

82
00:04:48,286 --> 00:04:51,809
spinner was even median quality. It's a missing role.

83
00:04:52,509 --> 00:04:55,943
They lost all three close finishes, but clutch doesn't

84
00:04:55,943 --> 00:04:59,314
repeat from season to season, so I read that as luck.

85
00:05:00,014 --> 00:05:03,969
Part five: what did the franchise do about it? I followed their

86
00:05:03,969 --> 00:05:07,673
decisions in order, keeping foresight apart from hindsight.

87
00:05:08,373 --> 00:05:11,284
Twenty twenty-three, what the data said, the

88
00:05:11,284 --> 00:05:14,261
December auction, and the two halves of 2024.

89
00:05:14,961 --> 00:05:18,201
At the auction RCB released both main spinners,

90
00:05:18,201 --> 00:05:21,304
which was fair. But of the new bowling money,

91
00:05:21,304 --> 00:05:24,475
thirty-six crore went on pace and two on spin.

92
00:05:25,175 --> 00:05:27,370
Their other weak spots moved between

93
00:05:27,370 --> 00:05:29,442
seasons. Middle-overs spin didn't.

94
00:05:30,142 --> 00:05:33,449
The arrivals produced more wins, but at almost nine

95
00:05:33,449 --> 00:05:36,691
crore per win, against a league rate of about six.

96
00:05:37,391 --> 00:05:39,936
There was no proven spinner in the auction;

97
00:05:39,936 --> 00:05:42,659
they were all retained elsewhere, so the route

98
00:05:42,659 --> 00:05:45,263
was a trade, like the one for Cameron Green.

99
00:05:45,543 --> 00:05:48,807
In hindsight, an established spinner in

100
00:05:48,807 --> 00:05:52,071
RCB's 2024 overs was worth about a win.

101
00:05:52,771 --> 00:05:56,750
And the 2024 turnaround, one win in seven then six in

102
00:05:56,750 --> 00:06:00,729
seven, came from the powerplay, not from fixing spin.

103
00:06:01,429 --> 00:06:04,636
The limits: two seasons give direction more firmly

104
00:06:04,636 --> 00:06:07,843
than size, shrinkage makes projected gains smaller

105
00:06:07,843 --> 00:06:11,114
than the measured cost, and the audit isn't causal.

106
00:06:11,814 --> 00:06:16,311
One top-quartile Indian spinner is worth about a quarter of

107
00:06:16,311 --> 00:06:20,732
a win a season on conservative estimates, up to nearly one

108
00:06:20,732 --> 00:06:25,382
if the past repeats. RCB missed the 2023 playoffs by one win.

109
00:06:26,082 --> 00:06:27,789
So, three recommendations.

110
00:06:28,069 --> 00:06:31,567
Move the middle overs now, to the bowlers already good there.

111
00:06:31,847 --> 00:06:35,265
Buy one Indian middle-overs spinner, judged on more

112
00:06:35,265 --> 00:06:38,951
than one season, with the bid capped by projected wins.

113
00:06:39,231 --> 00:06:42,047
And don't spend big on more pace, or on clutch.

114
00:06:42,747 --> 00:06:45,869
Everything is in the repository: three reports,

115
00:06:45,869 --> 00:06:49,190
the paper, the slides, the dashboard and the code.

116
00:06:49,890 --> 00:06:53,202
One note from outside the data: at the next

117
00:06:53,202 --> 00:06:56,745
auction RCB bought two spinners, Krunal Pandya

118
00:06:56,745 --> 00:06:59,981
and Suyash Sharma, and won the 2025 title.

119
00:07:00,261 --> 00:07:03,153
One result proves nothing on its own, but it's

120
00:07:03,153 --> 00:07:06,234
the gap the data pointed to. Thanks for watching.

